<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Judaism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jewish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Judaism is the monotheistic religion of the Jewish people, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and rabbinic tradition. Its history was shaped by the Assyrian and Chaldean conquests, the Achaemenid restoration and the conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> elite adopted Judaism, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> became a major centre of Jewish life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/judaism.jpg" alt="Judaism" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ariely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menorah_0307.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cyrus Cylinder has been celebrated in modern times as an early articulation of humane rule, and the Achaemenid treatment of the Jewish exiles in Babylonia, allowing the rebuilding of the Temple in Jerusalem, had lasting consequences for the development of <b>Judaism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The destruction of Jerusalem in 587/586 BCE and the Babylonian captivity profoundly shaped the development of <b>Judaism</b>, accelerating the shift from a temple-centred, territorially rooted religion to a portable, text-centred faith capable of surviving in exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jewish communities flourished and the court's monotheism drew heavily on <b>Judaism</b>, while Christianity spread in Najran and other towns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A distinctive feature of Khazar society was its religious pluralism, encompassing <b>Judaism</b>, Christianity, Islam and traditional Tengrist beliefs, which eased commercial dealings with diverse partners.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The prophets Isaiah, Micah, Jeremiah and others, whose words were preserved and expanded in Judah and in exile, developed ideas of justice, covenant and the sole worship of Yahweh that shaped <b>Judaism</b> and, through it, Christianity and Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The United Kingdom of Israel of Saul, David and Solomon, known almost entirely from the Hebrew Bible and debated by archaeologists, made Jerusalem a royal and religious capital, founded the House of David, and became the model of kingship and messianic hope for <b>Judaism</b>, Christianity and Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
